<section class="chapter" id="problem">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">1. Problem &amp; Purpose</h2>

      <h3>1.1 The Problem</h3>
      <p>Most ops improvement pipelines fail because:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ideas are scattered across docs, chats, and hallway conversations.</li>
        <li>No clear triage owner or prioritization framework.</li>
        <li>Experiments aren’t defined, so nothing is measurable.</li>
        <li>Decisions aren’t logged, so teams repeat the same debates.</li>
        <li>People stop contributing because “nothing changes.”</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>1.2 Purpose</h3>
      <p>
        Innovation A[i]gent provides a lightweight operating system for improvement:
        capture → prioritize → test → decide → document → measure → repeat.
      </p>

      <h3>1.3 Outcomes We’re Aiming For</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Higher throughput of meaningful operational improvements.</li>
        <li>Faster decision-making with less rework and debate.</li>
        <li>Clear evidence of what changed and why.</li>
        <li>Measurable impact on cycle time, quality, and experience.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="closure">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">2. Real-Time Idea Closure Loop</h2>

      <p>
        The pipeline only works if ideas reliably enter it. In most organizations, they don’t.
        This agent’s first job is to close the gap between insight and response — in real time —
        before delay, politics, or hierarchy erase the signal.
      </p>

      <h3>2.1 Front-Door Interaction (Chat)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Employee asks:</strong> “How do we do X?” → agent retrieves the current procedure and source.</li>
        <li><strong>Employee proposes:</strong> “Why don’t we do Y?” → agent checks institutional memory.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2.2 Outcomes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Previously tried:</strong> returns context (what happened, constraint, decision).</li>
        <li><strong>Already in progress:</strong> points to current owner/workstream and status.</li>
        <li><strong>Net-new idea:</strong> routes into intake with an initial “idea packet” (summary, rationale, suggested owner).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Why this matters:</strong> immediate closure prevents unnecessary negative sentiment and reduces re-litigation.
        Only net-new, viable ideas enter the formal triage pipeline.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="intake">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">3. Intake &amp; Triage</h2>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Low-lift by design:</strong> Intake should feel easier than writing a Slack message — but structured enough to avoid ambiguity.
      </div>

      <h3>3.1 Intake Sources</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Signals from Listening A[i]gent (themes, repeated friction).</li>
        <li>Ops retro items and post-mortems.</li>
        <li>Stakeholder requests (leaders, recruiters, HRBPs).</li>
        <li>Tooling constraints (ATS/HRIS limitations and workarounds).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3.2 Triage Categories</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fix Now:</strong> low effort, high pain, clear owner.</li>
        <li><strong>Experiment:</strong> needs a hypothesis + measurable outcome.</li>
        <li><strong>Defer:</strong> useful but not now (log why).</li>
        <li><strong>Decline:</strong> not aligned or unsafe (log rationale).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3.3 Prioritization (Simple Model)</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Factor</th>
              <th>Question</th>
              <th>Values</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Impact</td>
              <td>Does this materially improve speed, quality, trust, or experience?</td>
              <td>Low / Med / High</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Effort</td>
              <td>How hard is it to ship?</td>
              <td>1 day / 1 week / 1+ weeks</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Risk</td>
              <td>Could this introduce fairness, compliance, or privacy issues?</td>
              <td>Low / Med / High</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Confidence</td>
              <td>Is this validated (data + signal), or just a hunch?</td>
              <td>Single / Emerging / Confirmed</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Owner:</strong> Diane Wilkinson — design, prioritization model, and implementation of Innovation A[i]gent.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="guardrails">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">4. Guardrails &amp; Governance</h2>

      <h3>4.1 Governance Rules</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No experiments</strong> on protected classes or sensitive topics without review.</li>
        <li><strong>No automation</strong> without an override path.</li>
        <li><strong>Define success metrics</strong> before shipping changes.</li>
        <li><strong>Document decisions</strong> even when you say “no.”</li>
        <li><strong>Ship in slices</strong> and monitor leading indicators.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>4.2 Roles &amp; Responsibilities</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Role</th>
              <th>Responsibilities</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Ops Owner</td>
              <td>Runs triage, sets priorities, assigns owners, publishes decisions.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Experiment Owner</td>
              <td>Defines hypothesis, plan, metrics; executes and reports results.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>People Analytics</td>
              <td>Validates measurement approach; monitors for unintended impacts.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Legal / Privacy</td>
              <td>Reviews risk for changes involving data, automation, or monitoring.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="experiments">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">5. Experiment Design</h2>

      <h3>5.1 Standard Experiment Template</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Problem:</strong> what’s broken, and where?</li>
        <li><strong>Hypothesis:</strong> if we change X, we expect Y.</li>
        <li><strong>Success metrics:</strong> what will move (and by how much)?</li>
        <li><strong>Scope:</strong> which roles/teams/stages are included?</li>
        <li><strong>Risks:</strong> what could go wrong?</li>
        <li><strong>Rollback plan:</strong> how to reverse quickly.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>5.2 Measurement Examples</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Change Type</th>
              <th>Primary Metric</th>
              <th>Secondary / Guardrail Metrics</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Interview workflow automation</td>
              <td>No-show rate, time-to-schedule</td>
              <td>Candidate complaints, reschedule rate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Scorecard nudges / SLAs</td>
              <td>% scorecards within SLA</td>
              <td>Interviewer satisfaction, quality notes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Template / comms changes</td>
              <td>Reply rate, conversion</td>
              <td>Candidate confusion signals</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Process step removal</td>
              <td>Cycle time reduction</td>
              <td>Quality proxies, pass-through stability</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Principle:</strong> if you can’t measure it, it’s not an experiment — it’s a preference.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

